	abstract = {The extremal number of a graph H, denoted by ex(n, H), is the maximum number of edges in a graph on n vertices that does not contain H. The celebrated K˝ov´ari-S´os-Tur´an theorem says that for a complete bipartite graph with parts of size t ≤ s the extremal number is ex(Ks,t) = O(n2−1/t). It is also known that this bound is sharp if s {\textgreater} (t − 1)!. In this paper, we prove that if H is a bipartite graph such that all vertices in one of its parts have degree at most t but H contains no copy of Kt,t, then ex(n, H) = o(n2−1/t). This veriﬁes a conjecture of Conlon, Janzer, and Lee.},
